    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_09d6bc372bcfbddabd4d63cbab26ac4111d506e41c0a8348b6c4b5dbe50b1055",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "Lemma 4 turns evaluation on the logarithmically lowered line z=x-iκ log_+ x into a three-part analytic interface: a broad upper bound, a sharper exponential magnitude scale when κ is sufficiently large, and a logarithmic-derivative asymptotic. Together these are stated as key inputs to the next section's Riemann-von Mangoldt type asymptotics, but the span does not assign a separate zero-counting mechanism to each estimate. The gain is usable control of H_t away from the real axis; the cost is a logarithmic-height regime, because for t<0 no usable series for 1/H_t or H_t'/H_t is available at substantially smaller height. The later application still requires Λ<t≤0, although the analytic estimates themselves could be extended to bounded negative t.",
