    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_51a7044aef5b22ab89521c4161350c78d26e27d65c0f2a9e9e0bd6480befc4f3",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The source transfers smooth spacetime dissipation to a sharp terminal obstruction by spending both temporal and spatial slack. A favorable time and a multiscale-good interval are selected from the averaged renormalized energy; short-time propagation repeatedly shrinks buffered intervals while controlling environmental influx; the resulting time-zero block energy forces almost all normalized nearest-neighbor gaps toward one, contradicting an external positive-density small-gap theorem.",
